well today's figures do show that the  economy is experiencing a very strong  recovery fastest growth in the g7 group  of countries and evidence that our plan  for jobs is working but i'm not  complacent the shock that the economy  and our public finances have experienced  has been significant and it will take us  time to fully recover and that's why i'm  not done supporting businesses or people  and most importantly giving them the  skills and the opportunities they need  to find great jobs but more specifically  on the barriers to recovery i mean for  example would you say that we've  squandered some of our potential vaccine  dividend this summer because we've been  very cautious over things like travel  and isolation rules well on travel i  know the sectors had a very difficult  time but they've benefited from almost 7  billion pounds of support from start to  finish through this crisis and most  importantly over the past few weeks  we've been able to cautiously reopen  travel  for double vaccinated passengers and  that is an enormous boost to the sector  would you vote it sooner it was right  that we do these things in a measured  way and that's what we've done taking  advice as we go but now that we can see  you know a safe reopening of the travel  sector that will be an enormous boost to  them and hopefully safeguard many more  jobs obviously your priority will be  sustaining this recovery but there are  some big policy commitments coming up  that need to be funded not least social  care uh funding our climate change  commitments  how exactly are we going to pay for all  that given that the economy absolutely  is still recovering  well the economy is recovering and it's  recovering well i think when it comes to  the spending review over the the autumn  which we will do  what we're going to see is absolutely no  return to austerity people are going to  see very strong investment in public  services over the course of this  parliament for context by the end we'll  be spending 100 billion pounds a year  more every year than we did at the  beginning that's why we're going to be  able to have 50 000 more nurses 20 000  more police officers 40 new hospitals  and roll out things like broadband  across the country to spread opportunity  and and that's what we were elected to  deliver and that's what we're getting on  and doing but there are some big numbers  you know climate change alone 1.4  trillion pounds and it's time to level  with the taxpayers surely about whether  they're footing that bill well i know  people people will look at these numbers  and they will have concerns about what  that means so then what the government  is committed to doing is making sure  that we do the transition to net zero in  a way that does keep costs low on people  and what i'd say is look at our track  record over the past uh a couple of  decades we've grown the economy 75  and at the same time we've cut carbon  emissions by almost half so that shows  it's possible to do both things we can  become more prosperous as a country more  jobs more successful businesses more  money in our pockets whilst also  becoming greener and that's what we need  to do going forward as well the prime  minister and yourself are clearly  neighbours you live next door to each  other are you friends  yes we are  do you think we can afford all of his  promises yeah i think we're the prime  minister and i absolutely united in  making sure that we deliver on the  commitments we made to people that we  invest in public services that we spread  opportunity around the country but in  the short term we're united in making  sure that our economic recovery is as  strong and as quick as possible and  that's what my focus is and the numbers  today  show that my plan for jobs is working  and that is happening  the pandemic has clearly hit the poorest  the hardest and we know that and in the  last week we've seen fuel prices are  rising off gem raising the energy price  cap  and 